Flowers is comedy it's a sitcom that I  feel like tries to break out of its own  format it centers around a family and it  looks at I think that three main themes  are probably family and the idea of home  and mental illness it's set in its own  sort of world and a big part of making  flowers I think was us working together  try to build that world in a cohesive  way and recently I've started to wonder  if a helpful way of talking about it is  as a comedy wizard mental illness in  that if I'm with a funny person and they  start talking to me about I'm having a  hard time or about a serious issue I  don't think of them as not a funny  person I think of them as talking to me  about having a hard time it's always  quite hard to work out I think where an  idea comes from but one of the first  things that I remember about flowers is  having an image of the house in this  kind of slightly bleak landscape kind of  heathen landscape and that was I suppose  one of the key parts of building the  world of flowers was trying to find this  location which took a while and in many  ways is quite an impractical house to  film in because the ceilings are very  low in fact they're so low that Julian  he's quite tall Julian Barratt who plays  Morris the father has to stoop a little  bit on some of the levels and we often  say that the house is a little bit like  a character in itself the character of  shown I think was born out of my desire  to inject into the show a sense of the  Japanese comedies as that I grew up  watching as a kid I lived in Tokyo I was  eight years old and so some of my  earliest comedy memories are these  really silly Japanese sketch shows and  in the end it felt like probably the  easiest way to incorporate onto the show  was to create the character of shown and  then I think like all of the  other characters did he started to sort  of challenge his position in the sitcom  and sort of show me other aspects of his  character and I think he's partly a  comment maybe on the sort of comedic  representation of Asians in on the  Western screen perhaps in that I wanted  to sort of - I suppose present at first  a slightly stylized personality and then  to kind of evolve him into a  three-dimensional character I'm  extremely lucky I think to have had a  the opportunity to have made this show  and be to have been able to share it  with such an extraordinary group of  people and I think all over cast Julian  Barratt Olivia Colman Tony Rigby Sofia  DiMartino everybody are extremely funny  but also have depth and I think their  performances are key in our sort of us  trying to strike a balance between the  light and the dark between the comedic  moments and the more dramatic moments if  you like and the other thing to say I  think is that everyone who worked on the  show cast and crew were extremely a sort  of generous spirited and kind-hearted  and I think particularly on the second  series having made the first series  together there was a kind of sense of  automatic trust which really helped us I  think to navigate our way through the  story and to just sort of know that you  could rely on the other people to  support you in your decisions and I  think perhaps partly that was born out  of the subject material that the flowers  is is very much about family in  relationships and and the idea of home  but it's also in part I think about  mental illness and what I'd love the  audience to get from the show I think is  a sense of that warmth that I felt from  the people that I was making it with  sometimes I use the analogy of maybe of  working in music perhaps or  in a band or something we're writing the  music doesn't always preclude you from  performing it and if you want to have a  hand in producing it and mixing it and  that's something that you enjoy then  that sometimes happens as well and  obviously writing directing acting  they're all slightly different  disciplines but I see them in some ways  as part of the same thing which is just  telling storytelling and and making  something and so I see for me I've  always worked on some project in that  way so it doesn't it doesn't feel  strange to me and obviously it's an  extremely collaborative process from the  development of the scripts through  perhaps through the shoot through the  Edit into post-production it's you're  constantly having conversations with  heads of department would produce us  with the channel and I think what what  we wanted to do with flowers was to make  sure that we were always cross  pollinating ideas so that we could build  this cohesive world and it relied on us  I think just being able to communicate  and so you know I'm extremely supported  I think an affair extremely supported  both in a practical sense and in an  emotional sense and so I think the  people that I was working with make it  easier the only times that I find being  in a scene difficult when I'm directing  something is because often I feel like  you could judge a take partly on just  the chemistry of the of the seed and how  it felt to perform with the other people  and I find it difficult is when sureness  is playing very small part in quite a  big group scene or a complicated scene  because I can't judge it based on the  chemistry but I also have to make sure  that I'm standing the right place say my  lines so I can't observe it either and  so on the first series I would sometimes  write scenes where she would enter  briefly and then exit because I thought  that would mean that I could go from the  monitor into the scene back to the  monitor but the reality of filming means  that you know I would be running 200  yards Retta house by the time I got to  the monitor seemed to be over so  I tried to be mindful of that in the  writing of the second series as well as  in in a sort of on the shoot as well the  first series of flowers was set in in a  kind of wintery autumnal time and the  palette reflected that was quite a cool  palette there's the the second series  one of the first things I knew about it  was that I wanted it to be set in a  different season and I wanted it to look  slightly different so the palette is  warmer the colors are more vivid and I  think that was partly because I wanted  the first series to feel for the  audience like a kind of memory as it  would for the characters and so that was  one of the ways I felt I was trying to  achieve that as well as making the  characters look slightly different where  the first series looked into depression  a bit through the character of Morris  and the strains that I put on his  relationship with Deborah just who  Olivia commonplaces wife  the second series focuses a little bit  more on manic depression or bipolar as  it's known now known and I felt like  there kind of the warm palette the vivid  colors the music and the sound are a big  part of the second series I wanted it to  be a really sensory experience for the  for the audience and to try to give them  a sense of what it might feel like to  experience to experience hypomania I  suppose so I think the second series is  more ambitious in many ways both in a in  a practical sense and in a thematic way  and the characters I always say it feels  like that the characters have their own  agency and they're leading me to where  they want to go and they really do I  think take us to new places in the in  the second in the second series I'm  constantly research to write a script  and how hard it is to get a script to  the point where it feels like it was  easy and I think for me anyway it takes  it takes a lot of time it feels like it  it takes me showing up which is a  computer and just putting in the hours  and trusting that  even if it doesn't come immediately  which it rarely ever does that the  answer will come if I if I go into that  world and if I put myself in the shoes  of those characters and spend enough  time in there that the answers will  arrive and I think the people around you  are invaluable as well you know people  to bounce off people to ask questions to  people to feedback on the material  that's always I think even if you don't  agree with someone's opinion it's an  opportunity to improve the script and  not a sort of attack to be defended and  so I think it just takes time and just a  supposed a degree of faith like I think  I've got I've got used to the fact that  it's not going to happen straightaway  and that for me was almost what felt  like the sort of shift from doing it as  a kind of hobby to doing it seriously  was that okay I'm just going to put in  as many hours as it takes and the thing  is I think if you really want to do it  and you really love writing you will  happily sit there until it's done until  you're happy with it basically I love  directing because I think it's it's all  about the other people and it's about  getting the best out of the people  around you and listening to them and  it's such a delightful part of the  process for me because it just involves  a sort of having fun talking about ideas  and being surprised by you know other  people's input I think I think what  makes a good director is having the  confidence not to over impose your own  ideas or your own restrictions on the  actors and heads of department whoever  it is and to trust that you have your  sort of you have a reactive  responsibility to sort of try to get the  best out of them I really think that the  assembling of a team whether it's the  cast or the crew is is a huge part of of  any creative project you know on flowers  we were you know partly assembling a  team on  purpose of people who we thought would  be extremely good at their jobs and that  we thought we would get on with and but  I think there is also a certain amount  of luck involved and it's important to  try to make sure that you have a  chemistry in to sort of test that before  you commit to working together but then  how that evolves into an experience I  think is is partly down to being open  and being willing to be vulnerable with  each other and to trust each other and  to be able to be honest in a tactful way  and trust that it won't hurt anyone's  feelings but it's also you know like you  will be making a show directing is I  always feel like at kind of 90 percent  just problem solving because there's a  relentless it's just the sort of  challenges will be thrown at you every  single day and it sort of how you deal  with them if if you're able to sort of  handle them as a group work together to  sort of solve a problem without  panicking then I think that is like a  huge part of making it a success and  yeah I mean I've said it before but I  just feel extremely lucky on flowers  because I think we really had that and  we really did work together and that was  partly by design but also I think just  extremely good fortune  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]
 

